\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Automated scratch-wound quantification pipeline with monotonic temporal integration.} \textbf  {(A)} Experimental workflow. Confluent cell monolayers cultured in 96-well plates are wounded using a standardized mechanical scratch assay and imaged by phase-contrast time-lapse microscopy. Sequential images capture progressive wound closure from baseline to the final timepoint. \textbf  {(B)} Single-frame wound detection. Each frame is independently segmented using local variance--based texture analysis to distinguish the wound channel from the surrounding cellular monolayer. Candidate wound boundaries are refined through edge detection, RANSAC-based outlier rejection, and Savitzky--Golay smoothing before reconstruction of the wound mask. \textbf  {(C)} Temporal integration with monotonic closure constraint. Independent frame-wise segmentations produce wound-area trajectories, wound boundaries are modeled independently for each image column using a Kalman-filter framework with a hard monotonic constraint, ensuring that wound area cannot increase over time ($A(t+1) \leq A(t)$). This generates temporally consistent wound trajectories and strictly non-increasing area profiles. \textbf  {(D)} Quantitative metrics derived from the final segmentation masks, including wound area ($A(t)$), closure fraction ($f(t)$), edge displacement ($\Delta u(t)$ and $\Delta \ell (t)$), and closure speed ($v(t) = -dA(t)/dt$). The fully automated, training-free pipeline enables robust extraction of wound-healing dynamics without user-defined threshold tuning while preserving biologically consistent temporal evolution.}}{4}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:pipeline-overview}{{1}{4}{\textbf {Automated scratch-wound quantification pipeline with monotonic temporal integration.} \textbf {(A)} Experimental workflow. Confluent cell monolayers cultured in 96-well plates are wounded using a standardized mechanical scratch assay and imaged by phase-contrast time-lapse microscopy. Sequential images capture progressive wound closure from baseline to the final timepoint. \textbf {(B)} Single-frame wound detection. Each frame is independently segmented using local variance--based texture analysis to distinguish the wound channel from the surrounding cellular monolayer. Candidate wound boundaries are refined through edge detection, RANSAC-based outlier rejection, and Savitzky--Golay smoothing before reconstruction of the wound mask. \textbf {(C)} Temporal integration with monotonic closure constraint. Independent frame-wise segmentations produce wound-area trajectories, wound boundaries are modeled independently for each image column using a Kalman-filter framework with a hard monotonic constraint, ensuring that wound area cannot increase over time ($A(t+1) \leq A(t)$). This generates temporally consistent wound trajectories and strictly non-increasing area profiles. \textbf {(D)} Quantitative metrics derived from the final segmentation masks, including wound area ($A(t)$), closure fraction ($f(t)$), edge displacement ($\Delta u(t)$ and $\Delta \ell (t)$), and closure speed ($v(t) = -dA(t)/dt$). The fully automated, training-free pipeline enables robust extraction of wound-healing dynamics without user-defined threshold tuning while preserving biologically consistent temporal evolution}{figure.caption.5}{}}
